<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.4.1: Introducing the Zero Product Property</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02141</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.4.1: Introducing the Zero Product Property</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e9d7613a-4686-419d-8d71-55a3dab73aaf</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">What values of the variables make each equation true?</para>
      <exercise id="_8ad43010-4e4f-445c-9428-d077659343f1" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Enter the value of a.</para>
          <para id="para-00003">\(6+2a=0\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Correct! \(– 3\)</para>
          <para id="para-00005">Nice try! The correct answer is -3</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_5a986493-88af-432e-8330-00139cfbe17e" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Enter the value of b.</para>
          <para id="para-00007">\(7b=0\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Correct! \(0\)</para>
          <para id="para-00009">Nice try! The correct answer is 0.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_81cbe54c-941e-4369-a118-64ef330c2b6c" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Enter the value of c.</para>
          <para id="para-00011">\(7(c−5)=0\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Correct! \(3\)</para>
          <para id="para-00013">Nice try! The correct answer is 3.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_3c031f12-8a5b-4bc4-b1f4-bffffb2e1cd5" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">Determine which of the statements are true if \(g \cdot h=0\). HINT: There may be more than one statement that is true.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">Correct! The answer is (g=0\), \(h=0\), and \(g=h=0\). Either \(g=0\) or \(h=0\). But also, notice, if \(g=0\) and \(h=0\) at the same time, you can also say that \(g = h = 0\).</para>
          <para id="para-00016">Nice try! The correct answer is \(g=0\), \(h=0\), and \(g=h=0\) Either \(g=0\) or \(h=0\). But also, notice, if \(g=0\) and \(h=0\) at the same time, you can also say that \(g = h = 0\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00017">What did you notice about one of the factors in each of the previous problems?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00018">Enter what you noticed.</para>
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00020">At least one of the factors in each equation was 0 or equivalent to 0.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00021">We will use the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">zero product property</term> to help solve <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">quadratic equations</term> throughout this lesson.</para>
      <note id="note-00001">
        <title>ZERO PRODUCT PROPERTY</title>
  <para id="para-00022">If the product of two expressions is 0, then one or both of the expressions equal 0.</para>
  <para id="para-00023">If \(ab=0\), then \(a=0\) or \(b=0\), or \(a=b=0\).</para>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>